L wrote:
> Personally I think the fishing pole is a dumb and unworthy item
> because:
>
> * it creates items out of nothing (breaking internal logic)

I know what you mean, but I like using them anyway. I thought this YANI
would make them fit in a little better.
My YANI would reduce the "create items out of nothing" problem by
creating a monster rather than an item, and
monsters-generated-from-nothing is hardly new to nethack. Of course,
you'd still have fishypoles generating the odd pair of boots out of
nowhere but those are hardly game-breakers: More of a gag than a
feature.

Another way to remove your concern would be to make fishing poles
require fish hooks.  That way you are exchanging a finite resource for
food/ boots rather than just plucking them out of thin air.

> * its other functions are already performed by grappling hooks!
Well yeah, but i don't see anything wrong with duplicating functions in
different items, as long as the items do have distinguishing features/
abilities/ limitations.

> And inventing a unique monster type just so it can leave a corpse?
I believe this kind of thing has happened before: Am I right in
thinking that the 'human' and 'elf' monsters were made possible in the
game only because the zombies of those monsters would create corpses
and open up the possibility of creation via undead turning? (Look under
'type 4' of
http://www.steelypips.org/nethack/extinctionist-faq.html#t2.1 )

> Why not just create a "dead fish" item? It could come in various sizes,
> just as an iron ball comes in various weights.

Well, that's exactly where I started, but then I thought, what's the
difference between a "dead fish" and a "fish corpse"? Logically,
nothing, so why should the game treat them any differently?
Is there so much more code involved in the creation of a new monster
than there is in the creation of a new item? Granted, the fish as
suggested above would have some 'special case' coding attached to it,
but not a huge amount. Basically, i wanted to avoid the player asking
'Why does undead turning work on this dead goblin but not that dead
fish? Why does this dead tengu decompose but not my dead fish?' So then
I thought of creating "fish corpses" instead of "dead fish", and then I
thought, "well, when you catch a fish it's still alive" so the catching
live fish idea was born. It just adds new possibilities to the game.
For instance, you might starve while fishing because your pet keeps
killing and eating the fish before you can. Or imagine:

You catch a fish! --more--
The fish lands on a polymorph trap --more--
the fish turns into a vorpal jabberwock

>=-)

> Maybe catching a
> particularly huge fish could increase your endgame score.

Heh, nice. I like that one.



